hat the population is normally distributed. Therefore, the distribution of sample mean is also normal, and we have that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{\\sigma /\\sqrt{n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${\\sigma }^{2}$ is the population variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the original distribution is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $90%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.9=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>1.645)=0.05.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.05}=1.645.\n$$\n\nTherefore, a $90%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-67\\pm 1.645\\cdot \\frac{18}{\\sqrt{32}} \\\\ -67\\pm 5.234\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498508 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186498479 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498508 #edb/ref 1069 "q-142973" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498508 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498508 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/39/39a83b1081d22919c9549c4ff1c6ad8006dc802cc23f9802d762e97883bea48c.png)\n\nConsider a sample of size $n=12$ from a normal population with standard deviation $\\sigma =4.$ Given that the mean of the sample is $\\overset{―}{x}=9,$ find a $90%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the distribution.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>1.645)=0.05,$ where $Z∼N(0,1),$ as shown in the diagram above.*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498508 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2b3720b9-719b-5e5b-8310-cbdf4c3bbfe3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498508 #edb/ref 1074 "We are told 