.645\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{135}} \\\\ -2\\pm 0.142\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498543 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186498382 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498543 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143021" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498543 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498543 #edb/ref 1071 "The daily income of a supermarket is normally distributed with standard deviation $\\sigma =1\\,200$ dollars. During a particular $28$-working-day month, the mean daily income was $1\\,950$ dollars. Find a $90%$ confidence interval for the mean daily income $\\mu $ of every work day.\n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $Z∼N(0,1),$ the table below shows the $z$-scores ${z}_{p}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{p})=p$ for some particular values of $p.$*\n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498543 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "66172e40-7344-58fd-a3d5-83fcbd16bd0f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498543 #edb/ref 1074 "We are told that the population is normally distributed. Therefore, the distribution of sample mean is also normal, and we have that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{\\sigma /\\sqrt{n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${\\sigma }^{2}$ is the population variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the original distribution is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=1\\,950,\\,n=28,\\,\\sigma =1\\,200\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $90%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.9=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $z$-score value ${z}_{0.05}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{0.05})=0.05,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\n![](images/2f/2fc6327df99c6b88508b0c4de2e4edf6c5521974f05e2e2f02d09786937285a7.png)\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the normal distribution, we obtain that ${z}_{0.05}=1.645:$ \n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.05$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |\n\nTherefore, the $90%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1\\,950\\pm 1.645\\cdot \\frac{1\\,200}{\\sqrt{28}} \\\\ 1\\,950\\pm 373\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498557 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186498413 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498557 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143022" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498557 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498557 #edb/ref 1071 "In order to measure the acceleration due to gravity, Annie knocks a ball off a table and records the time it takes to reach the ground. The measurements of the falling time of the ball are normally distributed with standard deviation $\\sigma =0.2\\,s.$ She repeats the experiment $16$ times and obtains a mean falling time of $0.55\\,s.$ Find a $90%$ confidence interval for the mean falling time $\\mu .$\n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $Z∼N(0,1),$ the table below shows the $z$-scores ${z}_{p}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{p})=p$ for some particular values of $p.$*\n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498557 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "841adb19-e83e-5b6e-be60-db36b3558aba" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498557 #edb/ref 1074 "We are told that the population is normally distributed. Therefore, the distribution of sample mean is also normal, and we have t