rval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the distribution.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>2.576)=0.005,$ where $Z∼N(0,1),$ as shown in the diagram above.*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498559 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "87f55a60-f84e-5cb1-9665-2c11dc9713b6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498559 #edb/ref 1074 "We do not know the distribution of the population. However, the sample size $n=144\\ge 30$ is *sufficiently large*. Therefore, according to the central limit theorem, we may use the following approximation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{\\sigma /\\sqrt{n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${\\sigma }^{2}$ is the population variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the original distribution is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $99%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>2.576)=0.005.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.005}=2.576.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $99%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10\\pm 2.576\\cdot \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{144}} \\\\ 10\\pm 0.429\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498562 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186498367 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498562 #edb/ref 1069 "q-142985" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498562 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498562 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/64/6486d3914bb0f7b4898220512e96630d13d2b0f4a5c1851938f98371a4c0255f.png)\n\nConsider a sample of size $n=49$ from a normal population with standard deviation $\\sigma =1.$ Given that the mean of the sample is $\\overset{―}{x}=12,$ find a $99%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the distribution.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>2.576)=0.005,$ where $Z∼N(0,1),$ as shown in the diagram above.*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498562 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8fa7b8f1-fff9-5728-a4e4-0be7942c5cc6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498562 #edb/ref 1074 "We are told that the population is normally distributed. Therefore, the distribution of sample mean is also normal, and we have that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{\\sigma /\\sqrt{n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${\\sigma }^{2}$ is the population variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the original distribution is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $99%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>2.576)=0.005.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.005}=2.576.\n$$\n\nTherefore, a $99%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 12\\pm 2.576\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{49}} \\\\  & 12\\pm 0.368\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498573 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186498429 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498573 #edb/ref 1069 "q-142969" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498573 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498573 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/28/28f56656e191e342a4815ba74bdd29cba5247fba7d8ab0feca8ffbe0b785ab8c.png)\n\nConsider a sample of size $n=25$ from a normal population with standard deviation $\\sigma =4.3.$ Given that the mean of the sample is $\\overset{―}{x}=-5,$ find a $95%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the distribution.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>1.96)=0.025,$ where $Z∼N(0,1),$ as shown in the diagram above.*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498573 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b68ac86a-b851-54a2-a0bc-3666201fc505" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498573 #edb/ref 1074 "We are told that the population is normally distributed. Therefore, the distribution of sample mean is also normal, and we have that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{\\sigma /\\sqrt{n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${\\sigma }^{2}$ is the population variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the original distribution is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $95%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>1.96)=0.025.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.025}=1.96.\n$$\n\nTherefore, a $95%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & -5\\pm 1.96\\cdot \\frac{4.3}{\\sqrt{25}} \\\\  & -5\\pm 1.69\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498576 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186498362 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498576 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143123" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498576 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498576 #edb/ref 1071 "The amount of wine, in hectoliters, produced by one hectare of a vineyard in a particular region is distributed with a population standard deviation of $\\sigma =2\\,hl.$ A sample of $120$ hectares is examined, and it is found that the mean volume of wine produced by each hectare is $15\\,hl.$\nFind a $99%$ confidence interval for the population mean hectoliters per hectare $\\mu $ of the entire region.\n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $Z∼N(0,1),$ the table below shows the $z$-scores ${z}_{p}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{p})=p$ for some particular values of $p.$*\n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498576 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bb5fa69f-9c36-5007-b11d-0c6100a39ac6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498576 #edb/ref 1074 "We do not know the distribution of the population. However, the sample size $n=120\\ge 30$ is *sufficiently large*. Therefore, according to the central limit theorem, we may use the following approximation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{\\sigma /\\sqrt{n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${\\sigma }^{2}$ is the population variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the original distribution is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=15\\,hl,\\,n=120,\\,\\sigma =2\\,hl.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $99%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $z$-score value ${z}_{0.005}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{0.005})=0.005,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\n![](images/8c/8c5ac93fdb7b03a2bf9cad38c9e5687867a2fa2968120586f3bf2e6101c24cbe.png)\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the normal distribution, we obtain that ${z